omfortable lead, then played the endgame almost without loss (mean 0.7 points per move against the 14k's 1.2).", "moments": [{"turn": 12, "text": "White's F5 extended the center chain of E5, F5, F6 and F7, but it lost 5.3 points, and the engine preferred G5. Black's lead jumped from +2.3 to +7.6, the first real swing of the game, and Opus 5.5 took G5 next to build a wall down the G-file."}, {"turn": 15, "text": "Opus 5.5's H7 lost 3.6 points, with H8 as the engine's choice. White answered at H8, which itself lost 2.0, so the lead only slipped from +7.5 to +3.9 before recovering to +5.9."}, {"turn": 18, "text": "White's C4 pushed in between Black's C3 and C5 stones. It was the 14k's worst move, losing 7.8 points, and the engine's choice was B5. Black's lead went from +5.7 to +13.5, and the left side turned into a fight that White never won."}, {"turn": 27, "text": "Opus 5.5's B2 was its costliest move, losing 6.4 points against the engine's A6, and the lead fell from +13.9 to +7.5. The damage was limited: Black stayed well ahead, and the lead never dropped below +7.5 for the rest of the game."}, {"turn": 33, "text": "Opus 5.5's J8 cost nothing (-0.1) and was the engine's own choice. It began the clean-up on the right edge, where Black's J-file stones at J7 and J9 later sealed off White's H8 and H9 stones. From here the lead stayed between +10.0 and +10.6 until the end."}]}, "audit": {"by": "Sonnet 5.5", "verdict": "clean", "reason": "after.py places one move the player names and reports captures and every group's liberties, libs.py lists groups and liberties, and count.py counts stones and enclosed territory on a pasted board. Each works only on the given position, with no loop over candidate moves and no look-ahead; the player picked the candidates (G2, F3, E2, B2) and ran after.py on each by hand.", "stop": false}}